-- 06: async, events and concurrency (ADR 0004)
-- No `await` anywhere. Suspension Points: `wait`, `wait for`, `send … and wait`,
-- and Suspending Capabilities.

-- Fire and forget: goes in the receiver's mailbox, returns immediately.
on submit order
  send review with order to approvals
  -- ALT: send review(order) to approvals
  -- ALT: send "review" with order to approvals   -- HyperTalk sends a text message
  -- ?? `with` for arguments reads well for one argument. For several:
  send review with order, "urgent" to approvals
  --    …the comma-list ending in `to approvals` is hard to scan.
end submit

-- (Each section below is a separate take on a Handler, not a set of clauses.)

-- Request/reply: suspends until the receiver's Run replies; the reply is `it`.
on submit order
  send review with order to approvals and wait
  if it is "approved" then ship order
  -- ALT: put (send review with order to approvals and wait) into verdict
  -- ALT: ask approvals to review order                -- `ask` = send-and-wait
  -- ?? `it` again. HyperTalk readers expect it; but after a suspension, other
  --    Runs have executed, so `it` must be per-Run (it is: no shared state).
end submit

-- The receiving side
on review order
  if the amount of order > 1000 then
    reply "needs a human"
  else
    reply "approved"
  end if
  -- ALT: return "approved"          -- `return` doubles as the reply
  -- ?? `reply` vs `return`: with `return`, every Handler is a potential
  --    responder, which is simpler. `reply` would let a Run reply and keep going.
end review

-- Handler queueing policy: what happens when the message arrives while a Run
-- of this Handler is still suspended.
on tick, every time        -- run every one concurrently (default for ticks?)
  advance world
end tick

on save doc, queued        -- one at a time, FIFO
  ask storage to save doc and wait
end save

on keypress k, dropping    -- ignore arrivals while one is in flight
  handleKey k
end keypress

on search term, replacing  -- cancel the in-flight Run, start the new one
  send query with term to index and wait
  showResults it
end search
-- ADR 0019 / NOTES: the suffix forms below were agreed; the prefix forms
-- (`on every tick`, `on replace search term`) are gone.
-- ?? Prefix modifiers collide with commands: `on replace search term` looks like
--    a Handler for a message called `replace`. `drop` and `queue` too.
-- ALT (suffix): on search term, replacing
--               on keypress k, dropping
--               on save doc, queued
--               on tick, every time
-- ALT (block):  on search term
--                 when busy: replace
--                 …
-- The suffix `-ing` form reads best to me and dodges the collision.

-- wait
on blink
  show light
  wait 500 ms
  hide light
  -- (`wait until <condition>` dropped: use `wait for` an event.)
end blink

-- wait for: a one-shot subscription with an optional timeout.
on checkout order
  send pay with order to payments
  wait for paid {order: ^orderId} or 2 min
  -- ?? which branch fired? Options:
  if it is nothing then cancel order                 -- `it` = event, or Nothing on timeout
  -- ALT: if the wait timed out then cancel order
end checkout

-- Block form: mirrors `match`, and the branch is explicit.
on checkout order
  put the id of order into orderId
  send pay with order to payments
  wait for
    when paid {order: ^orderId, amount: a} where a = the total of order then
      ship order
    when failed {order: ^orderId, reason: r} then
      send refused with r to customer
    after 2 min then
      cancel order
  end wait
  -- This reads best of the three. `after <duration>` as the timeout branch.
  -- ?? Pin (`^orderId`) is advanced, but this is the most common use of it.
  --    The beginner spelling: `when paid {order: o} where o = orderId`.
end checkout

-- One-line form, for the common case
on greet
  wait for click from okButton or 30 s
  -- `from` takes a postfix-level operand, so the `or` is the timeout, not a
  -- boolean `okButton or 30 s`.
end greet

-- Delegation: pass the message along the Message Path
on keypress k
  if k is "escape" then
    close dialog
  else
    pass keypress
  end if
  -- was: `if … then close dialog` with `else pass keypress` on the next line.
  --    ADR 0019's single-line form keeps `else` on the same line.
end keypress

-- Decision-mode events: the Host asks "may this happen?" and the Script can veto.
on beforeClose door, deciding
  if the state of door is "locked" then veto "The door is locked."
  -- no `wait` allowed before this point (load-time error, ADR 0004)
end beforeClose
-- ALT: on closing door … veto
-- ALT: on close door, deciding       -- decision mode as a suffix, like `replacing`
-- ?? `before close` as a two-word message name breaks "handler names are one
--    word". `beforeClose` or `closing`?
